<SddReadiness keywords="readiness, setup, bootstrap, tooling, package-json, npm-scripts, gennady, stub, not-ready, proxy-handoff, sdd-readiness" type="directive" ver="2.0">
  <Mission>
    Bring a not-ready repo to the point where the SDD flow can run: a `package.json` exists and
    declares the five exact scripts `sdd-state` checks — `typecheck` · `test` · `test:coverage` ·
    `lint` (reaching `gennady`) · `format` — gennady is installed, and `READINESS` flips to `ready`.
    This is the minimal mechanical bootstrap, NOT the tooling design: a real command where a tool is
    already installed, else a visible TODO stub that points at the infra flow. Entered when
    `sdd-state` reports `READINESS=not-ready` — from the router preflight or from any skill's own preflight.
    Self-sufficient: it may run without the router core in context, so it repeats the operator-facing
    axioms it needs.

    Input — the `sdd-state` readiness verdict. Output — a `package.json` patched toward `ready`, plus a
    handed-over command list for anything that FAILED when the agent tried it (typically dependency
    installs the corporate proxy 403s).

    Out of scope: choosing the real tool stack with a Decision Log (the infra branch), and the project
    portal (the root branch). This flow only makes the commands EXIST and runnable.
  </Mission>

